.TH SINTERACTIVE 1 "July 2026" "sinteractive 0.1.3" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sinteractive \- start an interactive tmux session on a Slurm compute node
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sinteractive
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RB [ \-\-
.IR SBATCH_ARGS... ]
.br
.B sinteractive \-\-attach
.RI [ JOBID | NAME ]
.br
.B sinteractive \-\-cancel
.IR JOBID | NAME
.br
.B sinteractive \-\-list
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B sinteractive \-\-status
.RI [ TARGET ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B sinteractive \-\-refresh
.RI [ TARGET ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sinteractive
submits a batch job that launches a detached
.BR tmux (1)
session on the allocated compute node, then SSHes in and attaches to
it. Because the shell lives in tmux, the session survives SSH drops and
can be reattached later with
.BR \-\-attach .
.PP
Options not listed below are passed directly to
.BR sbatch (1),
so any sbatch resource flag (e.g.
.BR \-\-gres ,
.BR \-\-exclusive ,
.BR \-\-qos )
works as expected.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Launch options
.TP
.BI \-n\fR,\ \fB\-\-name\  NAME
Tag the session with a name for easy reattach. Allowed characters:
letters, digits,
.BR . ,
.BR _ ,
.BR \- .
The name appears in
.B squeue
(as job name
.BI sint\- NAME ),
in
.B \-\-list
output, and can be used anywhere a
.I JOBID
is accepted. Launching a second session with a name already in use is
refused.
.IP
Note that
.B \-n
is consumed by sinteractive as
.BR \-\-name ;
use
.BI \-\-ntasks= N
to set sbatch ntasks instead.
.TP
.BI \-\-node\  NODE
Request a specific compute node (alias for
.BR \-\-nodelist ),
e.g.
.BR compute01 .
.TP
.BI \-\-partition\  PART
Slurm partition (default:
.BR interactive ).
.TP
.BI \-\-time\  TIME
Wall time limit (default: 1 day). Accepts shorthand in addition to
Slurm formats; see
.B TIME FORMATS
below.
.TP
.BI \-j\fR,\ \fB\-\-threads\  N
Number of CPUs (alias for
.BR \-\-cpus\-per\-task ;
default: 2).
.TP
.BI \-m\  SIZE
Memory (alias for
.BR \-\-mem ),
e.g.
.BR 16G
(default: 8G).
.TP
.B \-\-mouse
Enable tmux mouse support: wheel scrollback, click to select panes,
drag borders to resize. Off by default because it intercepts terminal
selection; hold Shift to select text for an OS-level copy. With mouse
support on, tmux's own mouse selection copies out over SSH via OSC 52.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-mouse
Disable mouse support (overrides
.BR SINTERACTIVE_MOUSE ).
.TP
.B \-\-detach
Launch without attaching: wait until the session is ready, print
connection info (attach/status/run/cancel commands), and return.
Attach later with
.BR \-\-attach .
Useful for scripts and agents; combine with
.B \-\-json
for machine-readable output. Unlike a normal launch,
.B \-\-detach
is allowed from inside an existing session (it never nests tmux).
.SS Session management
.TP
.BI \-a\fR,\ \fB\-\-attach\ [ TARGET ]
Reattach to a running session by
.I JOBID
or
.IR NAME .
If multiple sessions share a name, specify the job id.
.IP
With no
.IR TARGET ,
reattach to your only running session. If several are running, they are
listed with ready-to-run
.B \-\-attach
commands so you can pick one; if none are, the error says how to start
one.
.TP
.BI \-\-cancel\  TARGET
Cancel a session by
.I JOBID
or
.IR NAME ,
reporting what was cancelled. Equivalent to
.BR scancel (1)
on the job, but accepts session names. Allowed from inside a session
\(em cancelling the session you are in ends it, like the wall time
running out.
.TP
.BR \-l ,\  \-\-list
List running sinteractive sessions with job id, name, node, partition,
elapsed time, time limit, and the session's current working directory.
.TP
.BI \-\-status\  [TARGET]
Show session status: state, node, partition, elapsed time, and time
remaining.
.I TARGET
is a
.I JOBID
or
.IR NAME ;
inside a session it defaults to the current session.
.TP
.BI \-\-refresh\  [TARGET]
Same output as
.BR \-\-status ,
and additionally asks the session to re-check its end time against Slurm
and rewrite its state file now instead of at its next poll. Use it after
changing a job's wall time with
.BR "scontrol update JobId=... TimeLimit=..." ,
so cached readers agree immediately.
.TP
.B \-\-json
With
.BR \-\-list ,
.BR \-\-status ,
or
.BR \-\-detach :
print JSON for scripts and agents.
.B \-\-status \-\-json
and
.B \-\-list \-\-json
both include
.BR end_epoch " and " remaining_seconds ,
so one
.B \-\-list
call can rank sessions by remaining wall time.
.TP
.BR \-h ,\  \-\-help
Show a short help message.
.TP
.BR \-V ,\  \-\-version
Show the version and exit.
.SH TIME FORMATS
.B \-\-time
(and
.BR \-t )
accept shorthand that is converted to Slurm's
.I [D\-]HH:MM:SS
format:
.BR 8h ,
.BR 30m ,
.BR 2d ,
.BR 1d12h ,
.BR 1h30m .
Values carry over, so
.B 90m
becomes 1:30:00 and
.B 25h
becomes 1\-01:00:00. Native Slurm formats (anything containing
.B :
or
.IR D \-)
and pure integers (minutes) pass through unchanged.
.SH DEFAULTS
With no flags, a session gets 1 day of wall time, 2 CPUs, and 8G of
memory on the
.B interactive
partition. Personal defaults can be set with
.B SINTERACTIVE_*
environment variables (see
.BR ENVIRONMENT );
explicit flags always win.
.SH WAITING FOR A JOB TO START
While the submitted job is pending, sinteractive polls the scheduler
every five seconds and reports why it is waiting \(em the Slurm pend
reason, e.g. waiting for free resources or behind higher-priority jobs
\(em together with Slurm's estimated start time, when it has one, and
how long you have been waiting. On a terminal this is a single line
that updates in place; when output is redirected (logs, scripts) a dot
is printed per poll instead.
.PP
Pressing
.B Ctrl\-C
while waiting cancels the pending job and says so.
.SH SUBMISSION CHECKS
Before submitting, sinteractive notes any sessions you already have
running and how to reattach to them, then continues \(em a reminder
aimed at the forgotten\-detach case, not a refusal.
.PP
Submission is refused, with an explanation, when:
.IP \(bu 3
the requested
.B \-\-time
would overlap an upcoming Slurm maintenance (MAINT) reservation \(em
Slurm would not start the job until after maintenance anyway. The error
suggests a shorter
.B \-\-time
that fits before the window. Passing
.B \-\-reservation
explicitly skips this check.
.IP \(bu 3
launching would exceed the interactive partition's per-user job limit.
The error lists your current sessions with reattach and cancel
commands.
.SH IN-SESSION KEYS
.TP
.B Ctrl\-b h
Open a help popup with job info (node, time remaining), detach and
reattach commands, and basic tmux keys. Any key closes it.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-b d
Detach from the session; the job keeps running. Reattach later with
.BR "sinteractive \-\-attach" .
.TP
.B Ctrl\-b $
Name (or rename) the session in place. Updates the squeue job name and
the name used by
.BR \-\-attach / \-\-status .
.TP
.B Ctrl\-b [
Enter copy mode for scrollback and keyboard selection. Copy mode uses
vi keys regardless of
.BR $EDITOR :
.B Space
starts a selection
.RB ( V
selects whole lines),
.B Enter
copies and exits, and
.B ?
searches up
.RB ( /
searches down). Copied text is pushed to the local system clipboard
over SSH via OSC 52;
.B Ctrl\-b ]
pastes into a pane. Press
.B q
to leave copy mode without copying.
.PP
Ending the shell in every pane
.RB ( exit )
ends the session and releases the allocation.
.SH STATUS BAR
The dot at the left of the tmux status bar tracks time remaining:
shown in the terminal's foreground color while more than an hour is
left (configurable via
.BR SINTERACTIVE_WARN_YELLOW ),
then pulsing yellow with a countdown, and a spinner blinking yellow/red
with an M:SS countdown for the final 10 minutes
.RB ( SINTERACTIVE_WARN_RED ).
The terminal bell rings once as the final countdown starts, so the
terminal tab is flagged even when you are looking elsewhere.
About ten seconds before the limit, a notice is printed in every pane
and the session shuts down cleanly, so the job completes instead of
being killed at timeout.
.SH SCRIPTING AND AGENTS
Headless workflows (e.g. Claude Code) can launch, inspect, and use a
session without attaching:
.PP
.nf
.RS
sinteractive \-\-detach \-n build \-\-time=8h \-\-json
sinteractive \-\-status build \-\-json     # incl. remaining_seconds
sinteractive \-\-refresh build \-\-json    # same, after a scontrol change
srun \-\-overlap \-\-jobid=JOBID \-\- CMD...  # run inside the allocation
.RE
.fi
.PP
Inside a session,
.B SINTERACTIVE_JOB_ID
(and
.BR SINTERACTIVE_NAME ,
if named) are exported, and
.B sinteractive \-\-status
needs no target. A state file (see
.BR FILES )
mirrors the countdown so the time budget can be polled without hitting
the scheduler. Do not trust a budget read earlier in a long-running
session: wall time can be changed underneath you with
.BR scontrol (1),
so re-read it before starting work that has to fit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Set personal defaults, e.g. in
.IR ~/.bashrc ;
explicit flags always win.
.TP
.B SINTERACTIVE_TIME
Default wall time (e.g. 8h, 2d). Default: 1 day.
.TP
.B SINTERACTIVE_PARTITION
Default partition. Default: interactive.
.TP
.B SINTERACTIVE_QOS
Default Quality of Service, passed as
.BR --qos .
Unset by default (no QOS is added); set it on schedulers that require an
explicit QOS.
.TP
.B SINTERACTIVE_CPUS
Default CPU count. Default: 2.
.TP
.B SINTERACTIVE_MEM
Default memory (e.g. 16G). Default: 8G.
.TP
.B SINTERACTIVE_TMUX
Path to the
.B tmux
binary used on the compute node. Default: /usr/local/bin/tmux.
Set this when tmux lives elsewhere (e.g. /usr/bin/tmux on clusters that
ship it as a system package).
.TP
.B SINTERACTIVE_MOUSE
.BR on | 1 | true | yes
enables mouse support. Default: off.
.TP
.B SINTERACTIVE_WARN_YELLOW
Seconds remaining at which the status-bar countdown turns yellow.
Default: 3600.
.TP
.B SINTERACTIVE_WARN_RED
Seconds remaining at which the blinking final countdown starts.
Default: 600.
.TP
.B SINTERACTIVE_GRACE
Seconds remaining at which the session shuts down cleanly.
Default: 10.
.TP
.B SINTERACTIVE_POLL
Seconds between end-time re-checks against Slurm, which is also how often
the state file is rewritten. Default: 30. Raise it where scheduler traffic
is a concern, at the cost of a wall-time change taking that much longer to
reach the cached file.
.PP
Exported inside a session:
.B SINTERACTIVE_JOB_ID
and, when the session is named,
.BR SINTERACTIVE_NAME .
.SH COMPLETION
A bash completion is installed by
.BR make " " install .
It completes options and, after
.BR \-\-attach ,
.BR \-\-status ,
and
.BR \-\-cancel ,
the job ids and names of running sessions. Targets come from the state
files in
.I ~/.cache/sinteractive
(see
.BR FILES )
rather than the scheduler, so completion stays instant when
.BR squeue (1)
is slow.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.cache/sinteractive/JOBID.json
Time-remaining state file, refreshed about every 30 seconds while the
session runs (see
.BR SINTERACTIVE_POLL )
and removed at teardown. Contains
.BR job_id ,
.BR name ,
.BR node ,
.BR end_epoch ,
.BR remaining_seconds ,
and
.BR updated_epoch .
.B $HOME
is shared, so it is readable from the login node too.
.IP
The end time is re-checked against Slurm immediately before every write, so
.B updated_epoch
is when the whole snapshot was confirmed: if it is more than about two
minutes old, treat the file as stale and fall back to
.BR \-\-status .
Age it exactly with
.BR "remaining_seconds \- (now \- updated_epoch)" .
A wall-time change made with
.B scontrol update JobId=... TimeLimit=...
appears within one poll interval, or at once with
.BR \-\-refresh .
When
.BR squeue (1)
cannot be reached the file is left untouched rather than restamped, so it
ages honestly instead of vouching for a budget nobody verified; the
status-bar countdown keeps running from the last known end time.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B sinteractive
1-day session, 2 CPUs, 8G memory.
.TP
.B sinteractive \-n myproj \-\-time=8h
Named 8-hour session.
.TP
.B sinteractive \-\-node compute01
Run on a specific node.
.TP
.B sinteractive \-\-time=2h \-p rna
2-hour session on the rna partition.
.TP
.B sinteractive \-\-mem=32G \-j 8
32G of memory and 8 CPUs.
.TP
.B sinteractive \-\-attach myproj
Reconnect after a disconnect or detach.
.TP
.B sinteractive \-\-attach
Reconnect to your only running session.
.TP
.B sinteractive \-\-cancel myproj
Cancel a session by name.
.TP
.B sinteractive \-\-detach \-n build \-\-time=8h
Headless launch for scripts and agents.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; nonzero if submission is refused, a target session
cannot be found or is ambiguous, or the session fails to start.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sbatch (1),
.BR srun (1),
.BR squeue (1),
.BR scancel (1),
.BR tmux (1)
.PP
Bodhi cluster documentation:
.UR https://rnabioco.github.io/bodhi\-docs/sinteractive/
.UE
